```java
class ParkingLot {
    private final List<Level> levels;
    private final SpotAssignmentStrategy strategy;

    Ticket parkVehicle(Vehicle vehicle) {
        for (Level level : levels) {
            ParkingSpot spot = strategy.findSpot(level, vehicle.getType());
            if (spot != null) {
                spot.assign(vehicle);
                return new Ticket(vehicle, spot, Instant.now());
            }
        }
        throw new LotFullException(vehicle.getType());
    }
}
```

`parkVehicle` stays five lines because it delegates the two hard decisions — which spot, and what
happens if none exist — to the strategy and to `LotFullException` respectively. Levels are tried in
order, so pair this with a strategy that already picks the best spot on a level; `ParkingLot` never
needs a global view.

```java
class Level {
    private final Map<SpotType, ArrayDeque<ParkingSpot>> freeSpots;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    ParkingSpot reserveSpot(SpotType type) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            ArrayDeque<ParkingSpot> bucket = freeSpots.get(type);
            return (bucket == null || bucket.isEmpty()) ? null : bucket.poll();
        }
    }

    void releaseSpot(ParkingSpot spot) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            freeSpots.get(spot.getType()).offer(spot);
        }
    }
}
```

The `synchronized` block is small on purpose — it wraps only the poll, not the ticket-printing or
payment logic that follows. A per-level lock, rather than one lock for the whole `ParkingLot`,
means two cars entering *different* levels never block each other; only genuine contention for the
same level's bucket serializes.
